{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Cursed be the man who makes a carved or molten image.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'Cursed' — from the time of making. And so it is written (Deuteronomy 9:21): ״ואת חטאתכם אשר עשיתם את העגל״ — 'and your sin which you made, the calf'; from the time of making, the sin was established." "<b>And sets it up in secret.</b> — until he does to it things in secret. From here our Rabbis of blessed memory said: an idol of Israel is not forbidden until it is worshipped, while that of a gentile is forbidden immediately, as it is said (Deuteronomy 7): ״פסילי אלהיהם״ — from the moment they carved it, it became a god, even though they have not yet worshipped it. And some say: these transgressions — he opened them with the matter of idolatry because it is weighed against the entire Torah as a whole. And after it, 'who dishonors his father and mother,' because the three of them — HaShem, father, and mother — are partners in creation, and therefore He warned concerning the honor of all three. And the reason Scripture singled out these eleven transgressions is that they are the ones that incur the punishment of exile, and therefore He specified them now upon their entry into the land." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): He mentioned here eleven transgressions because they are things a person can do in secret, and he began with the most fundamental, which is between him and his Creator, and afterward between him and his parents — for who knows whether he dishonors them? And so too 'removing his neighbor's landmark' is a hidden thing, and so too 'misleading the blind,' for the blind man cannot make known who misled him, and so too 'perverting the justice of the stranger, orphan, and widow' who have no helper — and this too is hidden, for if he perverts the justice of others they will protest against him and publicize it. And as for lying with a father's wife, his sister, or his mother-in-law, he is not suspected of being with them, and therefore it is a hidden thing. And it is not so with other transgressions. And 'lying with a beast' — for it has no mouth to cry out, unlike the case of lying with a male, therefore he did not mention it. And so too 'taking a bribe' — between the judge and the witness to testify falsely. Thus explained the sage Rabbi Abraham of blessed memory." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: these eleven transgressions correspond to the eleven things that David of blessed memory said in the psalm (Psalms 15): ״מי יגור באהלך״ — 'Who shall sojourn in Your tent' — that whoever dwells outside the land is obligated to perform them, and behold they are the opposite of these eleven transgressions. ״הולך תמים״ — 'He who walks blamelessly,' as it is said (Deuteronomy 18): ״תמים תהיה עם ה׳ אלהיך״. ״ופועל צדק״ — 'and works righteousness' — that he not rob and not take anything of his fellow's, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'righteousness — from your own, give to him'; and this is the opposite of 'removing his neighbor's landmark.' ״ודובר אמת בלבבו״ — 'and speaks truth in his heart' — that his mouth and heart be equal, the opposite of 'misleading the blind.' ״לא רגל על לשונו״ — 'He does not slander with his tongue,' the opposite of 'striking his fellow in secret,' which is going about as a talebearer. ״לא עשה לרעהו רעה״ — 'He does no evil to his fellow,' the opposite of 'perverting justice.' ״וחרפה לא נשא על קרובו״ — 'and a reproach he does not bear against his kinsman,' that is, that he not do with him a thing that bears sin upon it, the opposite of the uncovering of nakedness (arayot). ״נבזה בעיניו נמאס״ — 'Despised in his own eyes, rejected,' the opposite of 'dishonoring parents,' for the cause of dishonoring parents is that he considers himself wise and honored in his own eyes; but one who is lowly and despised in his own eyes will honor those who fear HaShem. ״כספו לא נתן בנשך״ — 'His money he does not give at interest,' for he does not covet the money of others. ״ושחד על נקי לא לקח״ — 'and a bribe against the innocent he does not take,' the opposite of bloodshed. ״עושה אלה לא ימוט לעולם״ — 'He who does these shall never be moved.'" "<b>And all the people shall answer.</b> He mentioned this in connection with idolatry, and in all the rest he mentioned and said 'all the people' — because of what they said: 'I' (אנכי) and 'You shall have no other gods' (לא יהיה לך) they heard from the mouth of the Almighty, and therefore ״וענו כל העם״ — 'and all the people shall answer.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :register {:flags {4 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:43.465682232Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}